#' AFNI Deconvolve
#'
#' Performs OLS regression given a 4D neuroimage file and stimulus timings
#'
#' @param STATmask Character; file path. build a mask from provided file, and use this mask for the purpose of reporting truncation-to float issues AND for computing the FDR curves. The actual results ARE not masked with this option (only with 'mask' or 'automask' options).
#' @param TR_1D Numeric. TR to use with 'input1D'. This option has no effect if you do not also use 'input1D'.
#' @param allzero_OK Logical. don't consider all zero matrix columns to be the type of error that 'gotforit' is needed to ignore.
#' @param args Character. Additional parameters to the command
#' @param automask Logical. build a mask automatically from input data (will be slow for long time series datasets)
#' @param cbucket Character. Name for dataset in which to save the regression coefficients (no statistics). This dataset will be used in a -xrestore run \[not yet implemented\] instead of the bucket dataset, if possible.
#' @param censor Character; file path. filename of censor .1D time series. This is a file of 1s and 0s, indicating which time points are to be included (1) and which are to be excluded (0).
#' @param dmbase Logical. de-mean baseline time series (default if 'polort' >= 0)
#' @param dname Character or numeric vector. set environmental variable to provided value
#' @param force_TR Numeric. use this value instead of the TR in the 'input' dataset. (It's better to fix the input using Refit.)
#' @param fout Logical. output F-statistic for each stimulus
#' @param global_times Logical. use global timing for stimulus timing files
#' @param glt_label Character or numeric vector. general linear test (i.e., contrast) labels
#' @param gltsym Character or numeric vector. general linear tests (i.e., contrasts) using symbolic conventions (e.g., '+Label1 -Label2')
#' @param goforit Integer. use this to proceed even if the matrix has bad problems (e.g., duplicate columns, large condition number, etc.).
#' @param in_files Character or numeric vector. filenames of 3D+time input datasets. More than one filename can be given and the datasets will be auto-catenated in time. You can input a 1D time series file here, but the time axis should run along the ROW direction, not the COLUMN direction as in the 'input1D' option.
#' @param input1D Character; file path. filename of single (fMRI) .1D time series where time runs down the column.
#' @param legendre Logical. use Legendre polynomials for null hypothesis (baseline model)
#' @param local_times Logical. use local timing for stimulus timing files
#' @param mask Character; file path. filename of 3D mask dataset; only data time series from within the mask will be analyzed; results for voxels outside the mask will be set to zero.
#' @param noblock Logical. normally, if you input multiple datasets with 'input', then the separate datasets are taken to be separate image runs that get separate baseline models. Use this options if you want to have the program consider these to be all one big run.* If any of the input dataset has only 1 sub-brick, then this option is automatically invoked!* If the auto-catenation feature isn't used, then this option has no effect, no how, no way.
#' @param nocond Logical. DON'T calculate matrix condition number
#' @param nodmbase Logical. don't de-mean baseline time series
#' @param nofdr Logical. Don't compute the statistic-vs-FDR curves for the bucket dataset.
#' @param nolegendre Logical. use power polynomials for null hypotheses. Don't do this unless you are crazy!
#' @param nosvd Logical. use Gaussian elimination instead of SVD
#' @param num_glt Integer. number of general linear tests (i.e., contrasts)
#' @param num_stimts Integer. number of stimulus timing files
#' @param num_threads Integer. run the program with provided number of sub-processes
#' @param ortvec Character or numeric vector. this option lets you input a rectangular array of 1 or more baseline vectors from a file. This method is a fast way to include a lot of baseline regressors in one step.
#' @param out_file Character; file path. output statistics file
#' @param polort Integer. degree of polynomial corresponding to the null hypothesis \[default: 1\]
#' @param rmsmin Numeric. minimum rms error to reject reduced model (default = 0; don't use this option normally!)
#' @param rout Logical. output the R^2 statistic for each stimulus
#' @param sat Logical. check the dataset time series for initial saturation transients, which should normally have been excised before data analysis.
#' @param singvals Logical. print out the matrix singular values
#' @param stim_label Character or numeric vector. label for kth input stimulus (e.g., Label1)
#' @param stim_times Character or numeric vector. generate a response model from a set of stimulus times given in file.
#' @param stim_times_subtract Numeric. this option means to subtract specified seconds from each time encountered in any 'stim_times' option. The purpose of this option is to make it simple to adjust timing files for the removal of images from the start of each imaging run.
#' @param svd Logical. use SVD instead of Gaussian elimination (default)
#' @param tout Logical. output the T-statistic for each stimulus
#' @param trans Logical. check the dataset time series for initial saturation transients, which should normally have been excised before data analysis.
#' @param vout Logical. output the sample variance (MSE) for each stimulus
